#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: production-v1
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: []
# doc: |
#   A method that does not exist on a PRIMITIVE receiver is a check-time
#   rejection, not something to lower — Core #10 lower-or-reject. `to_string` is
#   not a method of `float`: it is absent from the builtin-method oracle
#   (`src/semantic/typecheck.rs::builtin_method_type`, whose numeric/bool arm is
#   exactly `hash`/`debug`/`display`/`mod`) and absent from the IR
#   `GORGET_STRING_VIEW` protocol (`src/ir/lowering/builtins.rs`).
#
#   Accepting it is not benign. Before this pin the self-host ACCEPTED the
#   program and emitted C that would not compile — `incompatible types when
#   assigning to type 'Str' from type 'int'`, a `Str` destination minted from
#   the method NAME while the value is the raw numeric payload. That is the
#   silent-miscompile class the reject exists to close, and it is why the
#   verdict is pinned on all three production lanes rather than on the one that
#   already had it right.
#
#   adjudicator: production-v1 — `to_string` appears nowhere in ggdef's
#   `elaborate`, so its builtin arm-picker falls to the "outside the phase-0
#   subset" catch-all and the ggdef run-surface lane records GGDEF-SKIP rather
#   than MATCH. `ggdef gen` therefore cannot own this expect. The subset gap is
#   filed. The C, LLVM and self-host production lanes all affirm the reject at
#   check time.
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_NoMethodFound
#!end

void main():
    float f = 3.0
    print(f.to_string())
